WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:02.700
Instructor: Next step, we'll talk about creating tags.

00:02.700 --> 00:05.460
Now remember, there are two different types of tags.

00:05.460 --> 00:06.780
We'll talk about both of them.

00:06.780 --> 00:08.850
They're both very easy to make.

00:08.850 --> 00:12.180
There's lightweight tags, which are just a piece of text

00:12.180 --> 00:13.530
referring to a commit.

00:13.530 --> 00:15.690
And then there are annotated tags that have

00:15.690 --> 00:18.240
additional metadata aside from just a name,

00:18.240 --> 00:19.530
make a lightweight tag.

00:19.530 --> 00:21.480
They also include author's name and email,

00:21.480 --> 00:24.720
the date, and a detailed tagging message.

00:24.720 --> 00:28.050
All right, so here's how we make a lightweight tag.

00:28.050 --> 00:31.860
Git tag, and then some tagname, some tag we want to create.

00:31.860 --> 00:34.590
And by default it will reference, it will point to

00:34.590 --> 00:37.710
whatever the head is pointing at that moment in time

00:37.710 --> 00:40.800
or whatever the head is referencing at that moment in time.

00:40.800 --> 00:42.870
So basically the most recent commit

00:42.870 --> 00:44.340
on whatever branch you're on, right?

00:44.340 --> 00:48.600
If we run git tag, the tag refers to that particular commit.

00:48.600 --> 00:51.090
So if we're using react as the example here,

00:51.090 --> 00:55.460
if I git switch, I'll go back to master and what do we have?

00:57.450 --> 01:01.050
The most recent one, I think is 17.0.1.

01:01.050 --> 01:05.940
If I wanted to make my own new version, 17.0.2,

01:05.940 --> 01:08.010
I'll just patch something.

01:08.010 --> 01:09.450
What could we do?

01:09.450 --> 01:12.030
I don't know, we'll add a note to some file or something.

01:12.030 --> 01:13.290
This is gonna be silly.

01:13.290 --> 01:16.350
Here's the contents of the entire repository.

01:16.350 --> 01:18.510
I don't know, let's go into the "README"

01:18.510 --> 01:20.790
and just change something very quickly.

01:20.790 --> 01:23.370
JavaScript is an awesome,

01:23.370 --> 01:25.807
or "React is an awesome JavaScript library"

01:25.807 --> 01:27.330
"for building user interfaces."

01:27.330 --> 01:29.250
That's all we're going to do.

01:29.250 --> 01:31.050
And I'm gonna add and commit that.

01:31.050 --> 01:34.380
So I'm on the master branch, "git add README".

01:34.380 --> 01:37.200
git commit-m, I don't know, fix typo.

01:37.200 --> 01:39.650
It's not really a typo, but "Fix typo in README".

01:40.530 --> 01:44.790
Okay, so now if I want to tag this as a new release

01:44.790 --> 01:48.030
which I normally would not do, certainly not for that,

01:48.030 --> 01:51.660
but if I wanted to, I can use git tag.

01:51.660 --> 01:54.030
And if I just do git tag with a tag name

01:54.030 --> 01:58.620
like, we'll do 17.0.2, it's a patch.

01:58.620 --> 02:01.410
The most recent one was 17.0.1.

02:01.410 --> 02:03.930
So this is just logically the next patch.

02:03.930 --> 02:05.430
Anyway, if that's what I want to do

02:05.430 --> 02:09.090
I'll create a lightweight tag right here using the head,

02:09.090 --> 02:10.620
whatever head is referencing.

02:10.620 --> 02:11.940
Head references the master branch.

02:11.940 --> 02:14.370
The master branch, the tip is pointing at the commit

02:14.370 --> 02:15.330
I just made.

02:15.330 --> 02:16.163
Here we go.

02:17.430 --> 02:19.500
Well, it doesn't seem like much happened

02:19.500 --> 02:23.790
but if I do a git tag, it should show up down there.

02:23.790 --> 02:24.960
There it is.

02:24.960 --> 02:27.210
And I could keep moving, right?

02:27.210 --> 02:28.830
I could do some more work.

02:28.830 --> 02:31.680
Let's make another quick change.

02:31.680 --> 02:34.800
Let's say that we do another typo fix.

02:34.800 --> 02:39.510
I don't know, how about, "I like my pet chickens!"

02:39.510 --> 02:41.400
All right, so we'll pretend we've done some code.

02:41.400 --> 02:42.690
It's just another patch.

02:42.690 --> 02:44.280
So we'll do git status.

02:44.280 --> 02:46.860
I'll make a new commit, I'll do full screen.

02:46.860 --> 02:48.837
How about "update README".

02:49.952 --> 02:54.150
Oh geez, I forgot to do dash A if I wanna add that change.

02:54.150 --> 02:57.180
Okay, so I can create a new tag again

02:57.180 --> 02:58.860
using the current head.

02:58.860 --> 03:02.130
-: So I'll do "git log one line" just to remind you of that.

03:02.130 --> 03:04.500
Head currently on master.

03:04.500 --> 03:06.180
This commit, update README.

03:06.180 --> 03:08.880
So you can actually see my tag show up right there, right?

03:08.880 --> 03:12.360
That's the tag that I made earlier, 17.0.2.

03:12.360 --> 03:17.360
So now I'm gonna do a git tag v17.0.3.

03:18.720 --> 03:19.920
There we are.

03:19.920 --> 03:22.770
Okay, so I now have two tags that I've created

03:22.770 --> 03:23.970
in this video.

03:23.970 --> 03:24.930
There they are.

03:24.930 --> 03:26.490
They both refer to different commits.

03:26.490 --> 03:28.380
You normally, you know, wouldn't work on the master branch

03:28.380 --> 03:31.110
like I am anyway, but just to keep it simple and short,

03:31.110 --> 03:32.010
that's what I'm doing.

03:32.010 --> 03:33.360
And why don't we take a look

03:33.360 --> 03:38.360
at the diff between 17.0.2, the previous tag we made,

03:38.520 --> 03:40.440
and then the most recent one we made?

03:40.440 --> 03:41.370
And there we are.

03:41.370 --> 03:43.650
That's what changed between those releases.

03:43.650 --> 03:47.370
So that's how we can make basic tags, lightweight tags,

03:47.370 --> 03:49.830
which are actually kind of frowned upon,

03:49.830 --> 03:51.990
or they're not as popular as the alternative.

03:51.990 --> 03:54.810
We'll discuss in the next video the annotated tag

03:54.810 --> 03:56.763
which includes a bit more information.
